phone check; host or local? →Tier A personal-site-build-routes.\\n- **RTP CLA (9/10)**: NEXT: .ris → Zotero; lesson 0002. →Tier A rtp-cla-course.\\n- **ATH Tech**: shipped; ITS pending; run 9/29. →Tier A tech-inventory-refresh.\\n- **Status report**: #3 by 9/25, biweekly to 12/18 (timers). →Tier A supervisor-log-pipeline.\\n- **GPS pull (9/16)**: LIVE Mac 06:30; asleep=missed. DECIDE pmset 06:28 wake; rotate token. →`DataBase_GPS_Reporting/docs/STATUS.md`\\n- **Win refresh (9/13)**: ForcePlate broken, Perch untested. DECIDE which box owns those DBs. →`_INBOX/docs/STATUS.md`\\n- **FB matchups (9/13)**: EPA = PFF; CFBD PPA cross-check only. TRAP: `run_direction` != point of attack.\\n- **PP Recruiting (9/16)**: 010 + 011 matrix draft on Drive, pushed. NEXT: Eric reviews 011. →`PP_Recruiting/docs/STATUS.md`\\n- **Sleep scheduler (9/16)**: R1 MERGED. NEXT: night/early-kickoff itineraries. →`sleep-scheduler/docs/STATUS.md`\\n- **Research in 5 / TOS (9/18)**: #001 PR #7. #002 PR #8 NOT sent. TOS brief: DO NOT SEND. →handoff 2026-09-18-tos-evidence-review-and-ri5-002.md\\n- **TypeSafe (9/19)**: PASSES both boxes. Key per-box, NOT synced; needs `apikey_` prefix (401=key, 422=request). Full docs read; capability map MERGED to main (PR #2) and harvested. NEXT: skill router (hook must fail open). →`TypeSafe_Sandbox/docs/JEV_CAPABILITY_MAP.md`\\n\\n## Environment Notes\\n- Hooks: patch both `.claude/hooks/` and `.codex/hooks/`. →Tier A hook-two-copies-trap.\\n- daily-backup (9/19): 4 bugs fixed, 1 disproven. A manifest entry alone copies NOTHING; non-DEV paths need a Snapshot line too. →handoff 2026-09-19-backup-divergence-fix.md\\n- `DEV/Linda Projects` = private GitHub repo holding I-983 files. MUST stay private. Folder has a space, repo has an underscore.\\n- `~/DEV` = THIN repo; `.gitignore` starts `/*` — never remove. →Tier A github-consolidation.\\n- Schedules →automation-cadence. CFB capture (Mon, 8/24–1/23) →cfb-forecast-capture. Portfolio dashboard →command-centre.\\n\\n## Pending Decisions\\n- **OKR gate scored 9/14; still undecided**: AI integration buy-in + data governance (Anthony's proposal). Fold into NCAA charter or parking lot?\\n- **Kitman vs MyLink**: MyLink (~half Kitman cost) + Kitman API access. Undecided; blocks priority 3.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Cross-harness activity (KeystoneOS unified snapshot)\\n\\nNot loaded here — read it only if a question needs it.\\nPath: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/outputs/dashboard/unified-snapshot.md` (4122 chars, updated 2026-09-19 11:16)\"}}","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"Loading unified cross-harness snapshot...","durationMs":278},"type":"attachment","uuid":"56e696df-b8aa-4e06-8ad5-c99c449d77da","timestamp":"2026-09-19T17:24:41.429Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"fd1b25b8-4ce7-4809-8f8d-bd5c88dbfac4","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"session-start-progressive-disclosure"}
{"parentUuid":"56e696df-b8aa-4e06-8ad5-c99c449d77da","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"3565244d-3972-4f06-920e-5ac67c5728bb","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\"hookSpecificOutput\": {\"hookEventName\": \"SessionStart\", \"additionalContext\": \"## Today\\nSaturday, September 19, 2026\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Knowledge Base\\n\\n286 compiled articles (214 concepts, 71 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here \\u2014 read it only when a question actually needs it:\\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \\\"<query>\\\" --top-k 5`\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Recent Daily Log\\n\\n\\n## Memory Maintenance\\n\\n### Session (09:17)\\n\\n**Context:** Ran `/dev-sync` on the Mac (`~/DEV`) to reconcile local state with GitHub after Windows activity on 09/17\\u201309/18.\\n\\n**Key Exchanges:**\\n- Startup surfaced a `daily-backup` hook failure (`repo-push:dotclaude`); investigated and found the log line was `PUSH ERR | Everything up-to-date` \\u2014 a no-op push miscounted as an error.\\n- Advisor flagged that the first inventory pass reported repos \\\"clean\\\" without ever running `git fetch`, so the claim was based only on local state. Re-ran with fetch across all repos.\\n\\n**Decisions Made:**\\n- Picked `DataBase_GPS_Reporting` as the pick-up repo \\u2014 newest real commit; excluded `graphify-out` because its newer commits come from an automated daily job, not work.\\n- Committed `graphify-out`'s 10 generated-output files rather than leaving them dirty.\\n- Cloned `AppliedPerformance-Site` and `Linda_Projects` (missing locally, not archived) and asked Eric to confirm they belong on the Mac.\\n\\n**Lessons Learned:**\\n- **\\\"Clean\\\" is meaningless without `git fetch`.** The un-fetched pass claimed 140 clean repos; fetching revealed 5 repos behind. Always fetch before reporting sync status \\u2014 this is the single biggest failure mode of this skill.\\n- Sorting by commit recency picks automated-job repos (`graphify-out`) over real work; filter automation before choosing a pick-up repo.\\n- The `daily-backup` script has two bugs: it counts a no-op push (`Everything up-to-date`) as an error, and it writes every log line twice.\\n- `DEV/docs` and `SpecialRequests/docs` are doc folders inside parent repos, not standalone projects \\u2014 expected, not drift.\\n\\n**Sync result:** dotclaude +7 commits in, claude-skills +1 (Codex sync ran, 41 skills), global-memory +8, DEV root +2, Gemini_Projects +2, SecondBrain vault +2. 143 repos walked, 0 conflicts, 0 `.from-*` files. Clean.\\n\\n**Action Items:**\\n- Fix `daily-backup` script: stop counting `Everything up-to-date` as a push error; stop double-writing log lines.\\n- Confirm whether `AppliedPerformance-Site` and `Linda_Projects` should live on the Mac.\\n- GPS reporting open decisions (from STATUS.md, awaiting Eric): pmset 06:28 wake, Catapult token rotation, git-history rewrite to purge `API_Token_FB.txt`.\\n- Optional: have `claude-stack-doctor` read `last_run.json`.\\n\"}}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"Loading knowledge base...","durationMs":280},"type":"attachment","uuid":"9b28e903-5a69-47eb-b119-f2dbd00a8b06","timestamp":"2026-09-19T17:24:41.430Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"fd1b25b8-4ce7-4809-8f8d-bd5c88dbfac4","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"session-start-progressive-disclosure"}
{"parentUuid":"9b28e903-5a69-47eb-b119-f2dbd00a8b06","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"3565244d-3972-4f06-920e-5ac67c5728bb","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\"hookSpecificOutput\": {\"hookEventName\": \"SessionStart\", \"additionalContext\": \"## Today\\nSaturday, September 19, 2026\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Knowledge Base\\n\\n286 compiled articles (214 concepts, 71 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here \\u2014 read it only when a question actually needs it:\\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \\\"<query>\\\" --top-k 5`\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Recent Daily Log\\n\\n\\n## Memory Maintenance\\n\\n### Session (09:17)\\n\\n**Context:** Ran `/dev-sync` on the Mac (`~/DEV`) to reconcile local state with GitHub after Windows activity on 09/17\\u201309/18.\\n\\n**Key Exchanges:**\\n- Startup surfaced a `daily-backup` hook failure (`repo-push:dotclaude`); investigated and found the log line was `PUSH ERR | Everything up-to-date` \\u2014 a no-op push miscounted as an error.\\n- Advisor flagged that the first inventory pass reported repos \\\"clean\\\" without ever running `git fetch`, so the claim was based only on local state. Re-ran with fetch across all repos.\\n\\n**Decisions Made:**\\n- Picked `DataBase_GPS_Reporting` as the pick-up repo \\u2014 newest real commit; excluded `graphify-out` because its newer commits come from an automated daily job, not work.\\n- Committed `graphify-out`'s 10 generated-output files rather than leaving them dirty.\\n- Cloned `AppliedPerformance-Site` and `Linda_Projects` (missing locally, not archived) and asked Eric to confirm they belong on the Mac.\\n\\n**Lessons Learned:**\\n- **\\\"Clean\\\" is meaningless without `git fetch`.** The un-fetched pass claimed 140 clean repos; fetching revealed 5 repos behind. Always fetch before reporting sync status \\u2014 this is the single biggest failure mode of this skill.\\n- Sorting by commit recency picks automated-job repos (`graphify-out`) over real work; filter automation before choosing a pick-up repo.\\n- The `daily-backup` script has two bugs: it counts a no-op push (`Everything up-to-date`) as an error, and it writes every log line twice.\\n- `DEV/docs` and `SpecialRequests/docs` are doc folders inside parent repos, not standalone projects \\u2014 expected, not drift.\\n\\n**Sync result:** dotclaude +7 commits in, claude-skills +1 (Codex sync ran, 41 skills), global-memory +8, DEV root +2, Gemini_Projects +2, SecondBrain vault +2. 143 repos walked, 0 conflicts, 0 `.from-*` files. Clean.\\n\\n**Action Items:**\\n- Fix `daily-backup` script: stop counting `Everything up-to-date` as a push error; stop double-writing log lines.\\n- Confirm whether `AppliedPerformance-Site` and `Linda_Projects` should live on the Mac.\\n- GPS reporting open decisions (from STATUS.md, awaiting Eric): pmset 06:28 wake, Catapult token rotation, git-history rewrite to purge `API_Token_FB.txt`.\\n- Optional: have `claude-stack-doctor` read `last_run.json`.\\n\"}}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"uv run python hooks/session-start.py","durationMs":281},"type":"attachment","uuid":"d46c0746-9509-4e35-b372-b35f93efb240","timestamp":"2026-09-19T17:24:41.431Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"fd1b25b8-4ce7-4809-8f8d-bd5c88dbfac4","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"session-start-progressive-disclosure"}
{"parentUuid":"d46c0746-9509-4e35-b372-b35f93efb240","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"3565244d-3972-4f06-920e-5ac67c5728bb","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\"hookSpecificOutput\":{\"hookEventName\":\"SessionStart\",\"additionalContext\":\"\\n<context_window_protection>\\n  <priority_instructions>\\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\\n  </priority_instructions>\\n\\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \\\"timeline\\\")\\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\\n       - Each command: {label: \\\"section header\\\", command: \\\"shell command\\\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\\\"q1\\\", \\\"q2\\\", ...])\\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\\n\\n  <when_not_to_use>\\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\\n  </when_not_to_use>\\n\\n  <file_writing_policy>\\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\\n  </file_writing_policy>\\n\\n  <output_constraints>\\n    <artifact_policy>\\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\\n    </artifact_policy>\\n  </output_constraints>\\n  <session_continuity>\\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\\n  </session_continuity>\\n\\n  <ctx_commands>\\n    \\\"ctx stats\\\" | \\\"ctx-stats\\\" | \\\"/ctx-stats\\\" | context savings question\\n    → Call stats MCP tool, display full output verbatim.\\n\\n    \\\"ctx doctor\\\" | \\\"ctx-doctor\\\" | \\\"/ctx-doctor\\\" | diagnose context-mode\\n    → Call doctor MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\\n\\n    \\\"ctx upgrade\\\" | \\\"ctx-upgrade\\\" | \\\"/ctx-upgrade\\\" | update context-mode\\n    → Call upgrade MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\\n\\n    \\\"ctx purge\\\" | \\\"ctx-purge\\\" | \\\"/ctx-purge\\\" | wipe/reset knowledge base\\n    → Call purge MCP tool with confirm: true. Warn: irreversible.\\n\\n    After /clear or /compact: knowledge base preserved. Tell user: \\\"context-mode knowledge base preserved. Use `ctx purge` to start fresh.\\\"\\n  </ctx_commands>\\n\\n</context_window_protection>\"}}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"node \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/hooks/sessionstart.mjs\"","durationMs":281},"type":"attachment","uuid":"0e0f7e64-ff4b-455d-ad22-06af68f1558d","timestamp":"2026-09-19T17:24:41.432Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"fd1b25b8-4ce7-4809-8f8d-bd5c88dbfac4","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"session-start-progressive-disclosure"}
{"parentUuid":"0e0f7e64-ff4b-455d-ad22-06af68f1558d","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"3565244d-3972-4f06-920e-5ac67c5728bb","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\n  \"hookSpecificOutput\": {\n    \"hookEventName\": \"SessionStart\",\n    \"additionalContext\": \"<EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>\\nYou have superpowers.\\n\\n**Below is the full content of your 'superpowers:using-superpowers' skill - your introduction to using skills. For all other skills, use the 'Skill' tool:**\\n\\n---\\nname: using-superpowers\\ndescription: Use when starting any conversation - establishes how to find and use skills, requiring skill invocation before ANY response including clarifying questions\\n---\\n\\n<SUBAGENT-STOP>\\nIf you were dispatched as a subagent to execute a specific task, ignore this skill.\\n</SUBAGENT-STOP>\\n\\n<EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\\nIf you think there is even a 1% chance a skill might apply to what you are doing, you ABSOLUTELY MUST invoke the skill.\\n\\nIF A SKILL APPLIES TO YOUR TASK, YOU DO NOT HAVE A CHOICE. YOU MUST USE IT.\\n\\nThis is not negotiable. You cannot rationalize your way out of this.\\n</EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\\n\\n## The Rule\\n\\n**Invoke relevant or requested skills BEFORE any response or action** — including clarifying questions, exploring the codebase, or checking files. If it turns out wrong for the situation, you don't have to use it.\\n\\n**Before entering plan mode:** if you haven't already brainstormed, invoke the brainstorming skill first.\\n\\nThen announce \\\"Using [skill] to [purpose]\\\" and follow the skill exactly. If it has a checklist, create a todo per item.\\n\\n## Skill Priority\\n\\nWhen multiple skills apply, process skills come first — they set the approach, then implementation skills (frontend-design, etc.) carry it out. Brainstorming and systematic-debugging are Superpowers' most common process skills, but the rule holds for any of them.\\n\\n- \\\"Let's build X\\\" → superpowers:brainstorming first, then implementation skills.\\n- \\\"Fix this bug\\\" → superpowers:systematic-debugging first, then domain skills.\\n\\n## Red Flags\\n\\nThese thoughts mean STOP—you're rationalizing:\\n\\n| Thought | Reality |\\n|---------|---------|\\n| \\\"This is just a simple question\\\" | Questions are tasks. Check for skills. |\\n| \\\"I need more context first\\\" | Skill check comes BEFORE clarifying questions. |\\n| \\\"Let me explore the codebase first\\\" | Skills tell you HOW to explore. Check first. |\\n| \\\"I can check git/files quickly\\\" | Files lack conversation context. Check for skills. |\\n| \\\"Let me gather information first\\\" | Skills tell you HOW to gather information. |\\n| \\\"This doesn't need a formal skill\\\" | If a skill exists, use it. |\\n| \\\"I remember this skill\\\" | Skills evolve. Read current version. |\\n| \\\"This doesn't count as a task\\\" | Action = task. Check for skills. |\\n| \\\"The skill is overkill\\\" | Simple things become complex. Use it. |\\n| \\\"I'll just do this one thing first\\\" | Check BEFORE doing anything. |\\n| \\\"This feels productive\\\" | Undisciplined action wastes time. Skills prevent this. |\\n| \\\"I know what that means\\\" | Knowing the concept ≠ using the skill. Invoke it. |\\n\\n## Platform Adaptation\\n\\nIf your harness appears here, read its reference file for special instructions:\\n\\n- Codex: `references/codex-tools.md`\\n- Pi: `references/pi-tools.md`\\n- Antigravity: `references/antigravity-tools.md`\\n- Hermes Agent: `references/hermes-tools.md`\\n\\n## User Instructions\\n\\nUser instructions (CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, GEMINI.md, etc, direct requests) take precedence over skills, which in turn override default behavior. Only skip skill workflows or instructions when your human partner has explicitly told you to.\\n</EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>\"\n  }\n}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"\"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/hooks/run-hook.cmd\" session-start","durationMs":309},"type":"attachment","uuid":"a582df41-8468-49ad-bbb2-3f201ec8bde3","timestamp":"2026-09-19T17:24:41.459Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"fd1b25b8-4ce7-4809-8f8d-bd5c88dbfac4","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"session-start-progressive-disclosure"}
{"parentUuid":"a582df41-8468-49ad-bbb2-3f201ec8bde3","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_additional_context","content":["## User profile (global-memory/USER.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 1,375 chars. Updated when the agent learns durable user preferences. -->\n# User Profile\n\n## About\nEric Rash — Director of Applied Performance, Baylor Athletics. Builds athlete performance data pipelines (Football GPS, force plate). Family: wife Kristina; kids Ames, Saylor, Rowan (5, 4, 10mo as of 2026-09-01). Career target: analytics-forward player-coach near Iowa, ~$90K+ floor, ~mid-2027. Deeper context: `~/DEV/ContextVault` — read before career/personal advice.\n\n## Preferences\n- Python + R. **macOS since 2026-08-05**; Windows 11 box still ACTIVE (synced 9/9). Tool paths: see CLAUDE.md.\n- Local SQLite/DuckDB; no cloud/servers/schedulers without Baylor IT approval. Storage = Google Drive only (OneDrive dropped 9/13); DuckDB stays local — cloud sync locks it.\n- Tangible working examples over documentation.\n\n## Working Style\n- Direct; says \"stop\" when done. Wants to be challenged, not affirmed — push on vague or rehearsed reasoning (\"sharpened, not soothed\").\n- Defers technical micro-decisions when convention exists — decide, state it, move on.\n- Builds incrementally. Nothing that breaks mid-season.\n- Authors own direction when leadership doesn't; OKR-driven change agent in a status-quo org.\n- Right question over downstream craft; Christian faith is his organizing frame.\n\n---\n\n## Working memory (global-memory/MEMORY.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 2,500 chars. Working scratchpad; writes take effect next session. -->\n# Working Memory\n\n## Active Threads\n- **Prioritization slate (9/14)**: LIVE. 5 active, cap 5 (Fall). →Tier A prioritization-slate.\n- **Personal site (9/2)**: NEXT: phone check; host or local? →Tier A personal-site-build-routes.\n- **RTP CLA (9/10)**: NEXT: .ris → Zotero; lesson 0002. →Tier A rtp-cla-course.\n- **ATH Tech**: shipped; ITS pending; run 9/29. →Tier A tech-inventory-refresh.\n- **Status report**: #3 by 9/25, biweekly to 12/18 (timers). →Tier A supervisor-log-pipeline.\n- **GPS pull (9/16)**: LIVE Mac 06:30; asleep=missed. DECIDE pmset 06:28 wake; rotate token. →`DataBase_GPS_Reporting/docs/STATUS.md`\n- **Win refresh (9/13)**: ForcePlate broken, Perch untested. DECIDE which box owns those DBs. →`_INBOX/docs/STATUS.md`\n- **FB matchups (9/13)**: EPA = PFF; CFBD PPA cross-check only. TRAP: `run_direction` != point of attack.\n- **PP Recruiting (9/16)**: 010 + 011 matrix draft on Drive, pushed. NEXT: Eric reviews 011. →`PP_Recruiting/docs/STATUS.md`\n- **Sleep scheduler (9/16)**: R1 MERGED. NEXT: night/early-kickoff itineraries. →`sleep-scheduler/docs/STATUS.md`\n- **Research in 5 / TOS (9/18)**: #001 PR #7. #002 PR #8 NOT sent. TOS brief: DO NOT SEND. →handoff 2026-09-18-tos-evidence-review-and-ri5-002.md\n- **TypeSafe (9/19)**: PASSES both boxes. Key per-box, NOT synced; needs `apikey_` prefix (401=key, 422=request). Full docs read; capability map MERGED to main (PR #2) and harvested. NEXT: skill router (hook must fail open). →`TypeSafe_Sandbox/docs/JEV_CAPABILITY_MAP.md`\n\n## Environment Notes\n- Hooks: patch both `.claude/hooks/` and `.codex/hooks/`. →Tier A hook-two-copies-trap.\n- daily-backup (9/19): 4 bugs fixed, 1 disproven. A manifest entry alone copies NOTHING; non-DEV paths need a Snapshot line too. →handoff 2026-09-19-backup-divergence-fix.md\n- `DEV/Linda Projects` = private GitHub repo holding I-983 files. MUST stay private. Folder has a space, repo has an underscore.\n- `~/DEV` = THIN repo; `.gitignore` starts `/*` — never remove. →Tier A github-consolidation.\n- Schedules →automation-cadence. CFB capture (Mon, 8/24–1/23) →cfb-forecast-capture. Portfolio dashboard →command-centre.\n\n## Pending Decisions\n- **OKR gate scored 9/14; still undecided**: AI integration buy-in + data governance (Anthony's proposal). Fold into NCAA charter or parking lot?\n- **Kitman vs MyLink**: MyLink (~half Kitman cost) + Kitman API access. Undecided; blocks priority 3.\n\n---\n\n## Cross-harness activity (KeystoneOS unified snapshot)\n\nNot loaded here — read it only if a question needs it.\nPath: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/outputs/dashboard/unified-snapshot.md` (4122 chars, updated 2026-09-19 11:16)","## Today\nSaturday, September 19, 2026\n\n---\n\n## Knowledge Base\n\n286 compiled articles (214 concepts, 71 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here — read it only when a question actually needs it:\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \"<query>\" --top-k 5`\n\n---\n\n## Recent Daily Log\n\n\n## Memory Maintenance\n\n### Session (09:17)\n\n**Context:** Ran `/dev-sync` on the Mac (`~/DEV`) to reconcile local state with GitHub after Windows activity on 09/17–09/18.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- Startup surfaced a `daily-backup` hook failure (`repo-push:dotclaude`); investigated and found the log line was `PUSH ERR | Everything up-to-date` — a no-op push miscounted as an error.\n- Advisor flagged that the first inventory pass reported repos \"clean\" without ever running `git fetch`, so the claim was based only on local state. Re-ran with fetch across all repos.\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- Picked `DataBase_GPS_Reporting` as the pick-up repo — newest real commit; excluded `graphify-out` because its newer commits come from an automated daily job, not work.\n- Committed `graphify-out`'s 10 generated-output files rather than leaving them dirty.\n- Cloned `AppliedPerformance-Site` and `Linda_Projects` (missing locally, not archived) and asked Eric to confirm they belong on the Mac.\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- **\"Clean\" is meaningless without `git fetch`.** The un-fetched pass claimed 140 clean repos; fetching revealed 5 repos behind. Always fetch before reporting sync status — this is the single biggest failure mode of this skill.\n- Sorting by commit recency picks automated-job repos (`graphify-out`) over real work; filter automation before choosing a pick-up repo.\n- The `daily-backup` script has two bugs: it counts a no-op push (`Everything up-to-date`) as an error, and it writes every log line twice.\n- `DEV/docs` and `SpecialRequests/docs` are doc folders inside parent repos, not standalone projects — expected, not drift.\n\n**Sync result:** dotclaude +7 commits in, claude-skills +1 (Codex sync ran, 41 skills), global-memory +8, DEV root +2, Gemini_Projects +2, SecondBrain vault +2. 143 repos walked, 0 conflicts, 0 `.from-*` files. Clean.\n\n**Action Items:**\n- Fix `daily-backup` script: stop counting `Everything up-to-date` as a push error; stop double-writing log lines.\n- Confirm whether `AppliedPerformance-Site` and `Linda_Projects` should live on the Mac.\n- GPS reporting open decisions (from STATUS.md, awaiting Eric): pmset 06:28 wake, Catapult token rotation, git-history rewrite to purge `API_Token_FB.txt`.\n- Optional: have `claude-stack-doctor` read `last_run.json`.\n","## Today\nSaturday, September 19, 2026\n\n---\n\n## Knowledge Base\n\n286 compiled articles (214 concepts, 71 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here — read it only when a question actually needs it:\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \"<query>\" --top-k 5`\n\n---\n\n## Recent Daily Log\n\n\n## Memory Maintenance\n\n### Session (09:17)\n\n**Context:** Ran `/dev-sync` on the Mac (`~/DEV`) to reconcile local state with GitHub after Windows activity on 09/17–09/18.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- Startup surfaced a `daily-backup` hook failure (`repo-push:dotclaude`); investigated and found the log line was `PUSH ERR | Everything up-to-date` — a no-op push miscounted as an error.\n- Advisor flagged that the first inventory pass reported repos \"clean\" without ever running `git fetch`, so the claim was based only on local state. Re-ran with fetch across all repos.\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- Picked `DataBase_GPS_Reporting` as the pick-up repo — newest real commit; excluded `graphify-out` because its newer commits come from an automated daily job, not work.\n- Committed `graphify-out`'s 10 generated-output files rather than leaving them dirty.\n- Cloned `AppliedPerformance-Site` and `Linda_Projects` (missing locally, not archived) and asked Eric to confirm they belong on the Mac.\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- **\"Clean\" is meaningless without `git fetch`.** The un-fetched pass claimed 140 clean repos; fetching revealed 5 repos behind. Always fetch before reporting sync status — this is the single biggest failure mode of this skill.\n- Sorting by commit recency picks automated-job repos (`graphify-out`) over real work; filter automation before choosing a pick-up repo.\n- The `daily-backup` script has two bugs: it counts a no-op push (`Everything up-to-date`) as an error, and it writes every log line twice.\n- `DEV/docs` and `SpecialRequests/docs` are doc folders inside parent repos, not standalone projects — expected, not drift.\n\n**Sync result:** dotclaude +7 commits in, claude-skills +1 (Codex sync ran, 41 skills), global-memory +8, DEV root +2, Gemini_Projects +2, SecondBrain vault +2. 143 repos walked, 0 conflicts, 0 `.from-*` files. Clean.\n\n**Action Items:**\n- Fix `daily-backup` script: stop counting `Everything up-to-date` as a push error; stop double-writing log lines.\n- Confirm whether `AppliedPerformance-Site` and `Linda_Projects` should live on the Mac.\n- GPS reporting open decisions (from STATUS.md, awaiting Eric): pmset 06:28 wake, Catapult token rotation, git-history rewrite to purge `API_Token_FB.txt`.\n- Optional: have `claude-stack-doctor` read `last_run.json`.\n","\n<context_window_protection>\n  <priority_instructions>\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\n  </priority_instructions>\n\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \"timeline\")\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\n       - Each command: {label: \"section header\", command: \"shell command\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\"q1\", \"q2\", ...])\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\n\n  <when_not_to_use>\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\n  </when_not_to_use>\n\n  <file_writing_policy>\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\n  </file_writing_policy>\n\n  <output_constraints>\n    <artifact_policy>\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\n    </artifact_policy>\n  </output_constraints>\n  <session_continuity>\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\n  </session_continuity>\n\n  <ctx_commands>\n    \"ctx stats\" | \"ctx-stats\" | \"/ctx-stats\" | context savings question\n    → Call stats MCP tool, display full output verbatim.\n\n    \"ctx doctor\" | \"ctx-doctor\" | \"/ctx-doctor\" | diagnose context-mode\n    → Call doctor MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx upgrade\" | \"ctx-upgrade\" | \"/ctx-upgrade\" | update context-mode\n    → Call upgrade MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx purge\" | \"ctx-purge\" | \"/ctx-purge\" | wipe/reset knowledge base\n    → Call purge MCP tool with confirm: true. Warn: irreversible.\n\n    After /clear or /compact: knowledge base preserved. Tell user: \"context-mode knowledge base preserved. Use `ctx purge` to start fresh.\"\n  </ctx_commands>\n\n</context_window_protection>","<EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>\nYou have superpowers.\n\n**Below is the full content of your 'superpowers:using-superpowers' skill - your introduction to using skills. For all other skills, use the 'Skill' tool:**\n\n---\nname: using-superpowers\ndescription: Use when starting any conversation - establishes how to find and use skills, requiring skill invocation before ANY response including clarifying questions\n---\n\n<SUBAGENT-STOP>\nIf you were dispatched as a subagent to execute a specific task, ignore this skill.\n</SUBAGENT-STOP>\n\n<EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\nIf you think there is even a 1% chance a skill might apply to what you are doing, you ABSOLUTELY MUST invoke the skill.\n\nIF A SKILL APPLIES TO YOUR TASK, YOU DO NOT HAVE A CHOICE. YOU MUST USE IT.\n\nThis is not negotiable. You cannot rationalize your way out of this.\n</EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\n\n## The Rule\n\n**Invoke relevant or requested skills BEFORE any response or action** — including clarifying questions, exploring the codebase, or checking files. If it turns out wrong for the situation, you don't have to use it.\n\n**Before entering plan mode:** if you haven't already brainstormed, invoke the brainstorming skill first.\n\nThen announce \"Using [skill] to [purpose]\" and follow the skill exactly. If it has a checklist, create a todo per item.\n\n## Skill Priority\n\nWhen multiple skills apply, process skills come first — they set the approach, then implementation skills (frontend-design, etc.) carry it out. Brainstorming and systematic-debugging are Superpowers' most common process skills, but the rule holds for any of them.\n\n- \"Let's build X\" → superpowers:brainstorming first, then implementation skills.\n- \"Fix this bug\" → superpowers:systematic-debugging first, then domain skills.\n\n## Red Flags\n\nThese thoughts mean STOP—you're rationalizing:\n\n| Thought | Reality |\n|---------|---------|\n| \"This is just a simple question\" | Questions are tasks. Check for skills. |\n| \"I need more context first\" | Skill check comes BEFORE clarifying questions. |\n| \"Let me explore the codebase first\" | Skills tell you HOW to explore. Check first. |\n| \"I can check git/files quickly\" | Files lack conversation context. Check for skills. |\n| \"Let me gather information first\" | Skills tell you HOW to gather information. |\n| \"This doesn't need a formal skill\" | If a skill exists, use it. |\n| \"I remember this skill\" | Skills evolve. Read current version. |\n| \"This doesn't count as a task\" | Action = task. Check for skills. |\n| \"The skill is overkill\" | Simple things become complex. Use it. |\n| \"I'll just do this one thing first\" | Check BEFORE doing anything. |\n| \"This feels productive\" | Undisciplined action wastes time. Skills prevent this. |\n| \"I know what that means\" | Knowing the concept ≠ using the skill. Invoke it. |\n\n## Platform Adaptation\n\nIf your harness appears here, read its reference file for special instructions:\n\n- Codex: `references/codex-tools.md`\n- Pi: `references/pi-tools.md`\n- Antigravity: `references/antigravity-tools.md`\n- Hermes Agent: `references/hermes-tools.md`\n\n## User Instructions\n\nUser instructions (CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, GEMINI.md, etc, direct requests) take precedence over skills, which in turn override default behavior. Only skip skill workflows or instructions when your human partner has explicitly told you to.\n</EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>"],"hookName":"SessionStart","toolUseID":"SessionStart","hookEvent":"SessionStart"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"2a6dd083-102c-45d6-8f91-0bb93e289069","timestamp":"2026-09-19T17:24:41.460Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"fd1b25b8-4ce7-4809-8f8d-bd5c88dbfac4","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"session-start-progressive-disclosure"}
{"parentUuid":"2a6dd083-102c-45d6-8f91-0bb93e289069","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"44e017d5-3eff-4da8-b0de-3fbd4e8da040","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"Review the conversation context below and respond with a concise summary\nof important items that should be preserved in the daily log.\nDo NOT use any tools — just return plain text.\n\nFormat your response as a structured daily log entry with these sections:\n\n**Context:** [One line about what the user was working on]\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- [Important Q&A or discussions]\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- [Any decisions with rationale]\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- [Gotchas, patterns, or insights discovered]\n\n**Action Items:**\n- [Follow-ups or TODOs mentioned]\n\nSkip anything that is:\n- Routine tool calls or file reads\n- Content that's trivial or obvious\n- Trivial back-and-forth or clarification exchanges\n\nOnly include sections that have actual content. If nothing is worth saving,\nrespond with exactly: FLUSH_OK\n\n## Conversation Context\n\n**What you have now:**\n- **Rules page:** `SecondBrain/Projects/Applied-Performance-Operating-Cadence.md`. It replaces the old OKR playbook. The old OKR tracker is retired.\n- **The 5 projects:**\n  - Tech Inventory, for Kenny, due 10/9.\n  - HPI protocol, for Chris and Kenny, due 12/11.\n  - Education curriculum, for Student Services leadership, due 12/11.\n  - Return to Performance course, for Joe Smith, due 12/11.\n  - Women's health survey, for Andrew Gallucci, due 12/11.\n- **Parked:** NCAA best practices is next up and starts when a slot opens. 8 other work projects and 7 stale side projects are parked. Volleyball is marked done.\n- **Limit:** 5 active projects this fall only, then back to 4.\n- **Goals:** the 14 summer goals are scored, averaging 0.23. The fall goals are written, one per project, plus the status report and Noah's 1:1s.\n- **Timer:** a pop-up warns you on weekdays when you pass the project limit, miss a step, or miss a status report. On Fridays it also writes a weekly report to `SecondBrain/Inbox/weekly-slate/`.\n- **Calendar:** 65 two-hour project blocks from 9/15 to 12/11, placed in your focus time or open slots. File: Google Drive `00_Primary_Storage_Drive/05_SYSTEMS/ProjectBlocks/fall-2026-project-blocks.ics`\n- **Debrief:** `/interview-me` now asks which project you worked on and what moved.\n- **Dashboard:** republished at https://claude.ai/artifact/7YRejiYys1XPYJi5AFjhhX\n\n**What I checked:** all 240 registry tests pass, and the 3 timer test files pass. The calendar file has 65 events, each with its own ID. A check dated 9/19 correctly flags report #2 and Tech Inventory step 1 as overdue. All 4 repos are pushed.\n\n**What you do now:**\n1. Open the `.ics` file to import it into Outlook. Tomorrow has two blocks: HPI at 8:30 and Tech Inventory at 2:00.\n2. Send status report #2 by Fri 9/18. Then run `python3 ~/DEV/project-triage/portfolio.py mark-done \"report #2\"`.\n3. Agree the Tech Inventory summary format with Kenny by 9/18. Then run `python3 ~/DEV/project-triage/portfolio.py mark-done Tech_Inventory_Analysis --step 1`.\n4. Run `/interview-me` at the end of each workday.\n5. Copy `SSI_Notes_09092026.docx` from OneDrive to Google Drive.\n\n**Things to watch:**\n- The weekly-review pop-up has been dismissed for 45 days. The new warnings show up in that same pop-up, so either run weekly-review or retire it.\n- Blocks after 9/25 are placed around your recurring meetings only, because your focus time isn't booked that far ahead yet. Ask me to rebuild the calendar in about 2 weeks.\n- Please confirm the spelling \"Laurel Proulx\". Your files never mention the name.\n- Still undecided from the summer review: AI integration buy-in, and the data governance proposal from Anthony.\n\nresult: Built and pushed Eric's prioritization system: 5 projects with receivers and dates, a warning timer, a Friday weekly report, a project debrief in /interview-me, and a 65-block Outlook calendar file.\n\n**User:** 1. can you do this for me\n2-4. I will do these when needed\n5. please move to Google Drive for me.\n\n**Assistant:** Here's what you asked:\n1. Import the calendar file for you.\n2. Leave items 2–4 with you.\n3. Move the webinar notes from OneDrive to Google Drive.\n\nBefore I touch either one, I'll check two facts, both at once:\n- Which Outlook you have on this Mac.\n- That the notes file is where I expect it, and that the Drive folder for the NCAA project exists.\n\nMy plan for the file: copy it into that folder, confirm the copy matches, then remove the OneDrive copy.\n\n**Assistant:** Both jobs are done.\n\n**1. Project blocks are in your Outlook calendar.**\n- I added all 65 blocks to your main Baylor calendar through Outlook on the web.\n- Outlook confirmed the import.\n- I checked this week: HPI Tue 8:30, Tech Inventory Tue 2:00, Education Wed 9:30, and Return to Performance Thu 2:00 all show up.\n- Your calendar now shows those times as busy, so meetings are less likely to land on them.\n- If we rebuild the calendar later, delete the old \"Project block\" events first. Otherwise Outlook may add doubles.\n\n**5. Webinar notes moved to Google Drive.**\n- The file is now at `00_Primary_Storage_Drive/01_PROJECTS/_ACTIVE/2026-03-05_SSI_PerformanceTechRecs/SSI_Notes_09092026.docx`.\n- I checked that the copy matched the original, then deleted the OneDrive copy.\n- The NCAA project entry now points to the new location.\n\nThe temporary upload copy is deleted, and I closed the Chrome tab.\n\nresult: Added the 65 fall project blocks to Eric's Outlook work calendar (import confirmed, this week's blocks visible) and moved SSI_Notes_09092026.docx from OneDrive to the Google Drive NCAA project folder.\n\n**User:** <command-message>closeout</command-message>\n<command-name>/closeout</command-name>\n\n**User:** Base directory for this skill: /Users/kristinarash/.claude/skills/closeout\n\n# Closeout\n\nOne command that replaces the manual end-of-day sequence: \"any loose ends?\" → \"commit and push what needs to be\" → \"is this in memory?\" → \"write a handoff so Codex can pick this up.\"\n\nRun all five checks, then give ONE verdict. Do not ask permission between steps — the invocation of this skill IS the authorization for the full sequence. Commit and push is standing-authorized for every DEV repo: each repo's `AGENTS.md` Session routine says \"update STATUS, commit, push at end\", and unpushed work on one machine is the top cause of conflicts on the other. The only exceptions are repos whose `CLAUDE.md` says their data must stay local (DexaProject is the precedent); commit those locally and list them as open.\n\n## Step 1 — Loose-ends sweep\n\n- `git status` across **every** repo in DEV, not only the ones named in this conversation. Another session, another harness, or the other machine may have left work dirty, and a closeout that only checks what it remembers touching will say \"clear to sign off\" over someone else's uncommitted work. Walk `DEV/`, `DEV/Football/` and `DEV/SpecialRequests/`, and report any repo with uncommitted or unpushed commits. The full walk takes about 90 seconds.\n  - Windows: `git -C <repo> status --porcelain` per repo. Do NOT use `Get-ChildItem -Recurse` to look for files; it silently skips paths past the 260-character limit. Inside a repo, `git ls-files` is the reliable way to ask whether a file exists.\n  - Repos with dirty state you did not touch: commit and push them like any other, and name them in the report so the user knows another session left them.\n- Unfinished todos/tasks from this session (TaskList if available).\n- Background jobs or workflows still running.\n- Anything the user was promised earlier in the session that wasn't delivered.\n\n## Step 2 — Commit & push\n\n- Every repo touched this session: stage, commit with a descriptive message, `git pull --no-rebase` (the other machine may have pushed), push. If the merge conflicts, follow the conflict steps in `dev-sync/SKILL.md` (they are the one home for them), then push and list the file under \"Still open\".\n- Before staging, make sure any new file over 95 MB and any `.env` is in `.gitignore`.\n- Local-only repos (CLAUDE.md says so): commit, do not push, list it.\n- Also check the three config repos, `~/.claude` (dotclaude), `~/.claude/skills` (claude-skills) and `~/.claude/global-memory` (claude-global-memory). If any has changes, commit and push it the same way, so the other machine's `/dev-sync` picks it up.\n- **Push `global-memory` even on a light session.** `daily-backup` pushes it nightly, which is too late for a same-day handoff: work the Mac in the afternoon, sit down at Windows that evening, and anything written to MEMORY.md or handoffs is still only on the Mac. Pushing it here is what makes memory available to the other machine tonight rather than tomorrow. It is the only repo that carries memory across, so treat an unpushed `global-memory` the same as an unpushed project repo: it blocks \"clear to sign off\".\n- Nothing to commit → say so in one line, move on.\n\n## Step 3 — Memory verification\n\n- Check `~/.claude/global-memory/transcripts/.errors.log` — must be empty. If not, surface contents and fix the hook issue if obvious.\n- Confirm today's transcript file exists at `~/.claude/global-memory/transcripts/<today>.md` and was modified within the last few hours.\n- Run the dev-docs harvest so repo docs reach memsearch: `bash ~/.claude/scripts/harvest-dev-docs.sh` (Mac) or `powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File $HOME\\.claude\\scripts\\harvest-dev-docs.ps1` (Windows). It copies every repo's `docs/*.md` into `~/.claude/global-memory/dev-docs/`, which is on the index list. One line of output; do this after Step 4 so today's STATUS is included.\n- If this session produced a durable decision or new active thread, update `~/.claude/global-memory/MEMORY.md` (respect the 2,500-char cap; consolidate before adding) or write a Tier A memory file per the memory-system rules in CLAUDE.md.\n- **Tier A does not cross machines — mirror it or lose it.** Project memory lives under a slug built from the project's absolute path, and that path differs on the Mac and the Windows laptop, so a Tier A file written today is not auto-injected tomorrow on the other machine. Global memory and the repo both cross; Tier A does not. So for every Tier A fact written this session, ask: is this durable and project-scoped? If yes, it must also land in the repo — `docs/STATUS.md` for current state, `docs/DECISIONS.md` for a decision and its reasoning, the dated handoff for the detail. Treat Tier A as a fast local cache and the repo as the record. If a fact only ever exists in Tier A, assume the other machine will never see it.\n\n## Step 4 — Cross-harness handoff\n\nThis is the **canonical durable handoff** — the one future sessions and other harnesses read. (For a mid-session, chat-only summary before `/clear`, that's the `session-handoff` skill; the mattpocock `handoff` skill writes to the OS temp dir and is not durable — don't rely on it for cross-session state.)\n\nIf real work happened this session (not just Q&A), write a handoff to\n`<repo>/docs/handoffs/<YYYY-MM-DD>-<slug>.md` (or `~/.claude/global-memory/handoffs/` if no repo fits):\n\n- What was being worked on and why\n- Decisions made (with the reasoning that would be lost)\n- Exact file paths of everything created or modified this session\n- Next actions, most important first\n- Anything mid-flight (open questions, awaited inputs)\n\nPlain markdown, no harness-specific syntax — Codex, Antigravity, or a fresh Claude session must be able to resume from it. Commit it with the Step 2 commit when in a repo.\n\n**Always, in every repo touched (even for light sessions): update `docs/STATUS.md`.** It is the file the next session reads first, on either machine, in any harness. Rewrite the sections in place, keep it short:\n\n- `## Current state` — two to five lines on where things stand today\n- `## Next` — most important first\n- `## Open questions` — decisions waiting on Eric\n- `## Gotchas` — anything that will bite the next session\n\nThe dated handoff file is the detailed record; STATUS.md is the summary. Do Step 4 before the Step 2 commit so both land in the same push.\n\n## Step 5 — Verdict\n\nEnd with a single short report:\n\n```\n## Closeout — <date>\n\n**Committed & pushed:** <repos + one-line summary, or \"nothing to commit\">\n**STATUS.md:** updated in <repos>\n**Memory:** captured ✓ / issue found: <what>\n**Handoff:** <path, or \"not needed — no substantive work\">\n**Still open:** <numbered list, or \"none\">\n\nClear to sign off. / Not clear: <the one thing blocking>.\n```\n\n\"Clear to sign off\" means: every touched repo is pushed (or is a declared local-only repo). If anything is unpushed, the verdict is \"Not clear\" and the unpushed repo is the one thing blocking.\n\nEvery file created or modified during the session must appear somewhere in the report with its full path — \"where did this save?\" should never need to be asked.\n\n## Rules\n\n- Push every touched DEV repo. Do not leave the laptop with unpushed work; the other machine pulls from GitHub, not from this disk.\n- Never skip Step 3 — memory capture failing silently is the user's top recurring worry.\n- If a check fails and the fix is quick and safe (restage a file, clear a stale lock), fix it and note it. If the fix is risky, list it under \"Still open\" instead.\n- Total output should fit on one screen. The user is leaving; brevity is the deliverable.\n\n**Assistant:** Using the closeout skill to finish the session. First, two checks that don't depend on each other:\n- Git status for every repo in DEV, including Football and SpecialRequests, plus the three config repos.\n- The memory hook error log, and whether today's transcript file exists.\n\nNo background jobs are still running from this session.\n\n**Assistant:** ## Closeout — 2026-09-14\n\n**Committed & pushed:** every repo is pushed and matches GitHub.\n- `~/DEV/project-triage`: slate check, `mark-done`, weekly report, calendar builder, tests, the project list, the handoff and the status file.\n- `~/DEV/KeystoneOS`: the timer now checks the slate and writes the Friday report.\n- `~/SecondBrain`: the rules page, the scored and new goals, the active-projects list and the status file.\n- `~/.claude/skills`: the project-block question added to `/interview-me`.\n- `~/.claude` config: 49 changes, mostly session logs from other sessions. No secrets were found.\n- `~/.claude/global-memory`: `MEMORY.md`, today's transcript and the dev-docs harvest (137 repos).\n\n**STATUS.md:** updated in project-triage, KeystoneOS and SecondBrain.\n\n**Memory:** captured ✓\n- The error log is empty.\n- Today's transcript was saved at 22:00.\n- The project memory note is `~/.claude/projects/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/memory/project_prioritization-slate.md`. Its facts are also copied into the handoff and project-triage's status file, so the other machine sees them.\n\n**Handoff:** `~/DEV/project-triage/docs/handoffs/2026-09-14-prioritization-system.md`\n\n**Other files:**\n- Calendar file: Drive `00_Primary_Storage_Drive/05_SYSTEMS/ProjectBlocks/fall-2026-project-blocks.ics`. It is already imported into Outlook.\n- Webinar notes: Drive `01_PROJECTS/_ACTIVE/2026-03-05_SSI_PerformanceTechRecs/SSI_Notes_09092026.docx`.\n- Dashboard: https://claude.ai/artifact/7YRejiYys1XPYJi5AFjhhX\n\n**Still open:**\n1. Send status report #2 and agree the Tech Inventory plan with Kenny. Both are due Fri 9/18.\n2. Rebuild the calendar blocks around 9/28.\n3. Confirm the spelling \"Laurel Proulx\".\n4. Weekly-review is 45 days overdue. Run it or retire it.\n5. Two decisions are still waiting from the summer review: AI integration buy-in and the data governance proposal.\n\nClear to sign off.\n\nresult: Closeout done. All 6 repos are pushed, the status files are updated, memory is saved, and the handoff is at ~/DEV/project-triage/docs/handoffs/2026-09-14-prioritization-system.md.\n\n**User:** stop\n\n**Assistant:** Okay, I've stopped. The closeout finished, so everything from today is saved and pushed."},"uuid":"5c2326b9-7056-47ae-bfb7-8e3cf5ed0c5d","timestamp":"2026-09-19T17:24:42.870Z","permissionMode":"auto","promptSource":"sdk","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"fd1b25b8-4ce7-4809-8f8d-bd5c88dbfac4","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"session-start-progressive-disclosure"}
